# What must never reach a build context.
#
# The Dockerfile does `COPY . .`, so anything not excluded here is copied into
# an image layer and stays there even if a later step deletes it. The first
# three entries are the ones that matter: a .env carries the session secret and
# the field encryption key, and an image built with those inside is an image
# that must never be shared.

# Docker matches these against the path from the context root, so a bare
# ".env" would leave deploy/docker/.env sitting in the build context. The
# **/ forms are the ones doing the work.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example
!**/env.example
*.pem
**/*.pem
*.key
**/*.key
license.key
**/license.key

# Local state. Never wanted in an image, and copying it makes builds slow.
node_modules
**/node_modules
.pnpm-store
dist
apps/api/dist
apps/api/data
apps/api/.tmp
apps/api/logs
apps/api/backups
apps/api/evidence
data
logs
backups
evidence
.local

# History and tooling.
.git
.gitignore
.github
.vscode
.idea
*.log

# Documentation and deployment scripts: none of it runs in the container.
docs
deploy/windows
*.md
!README.md

# Test output.
coverage

# TypeScript's incremental build state. This one is not housekeeping: with
# `incremental: true`, a .tsbuildinfo copied in from the host tells tsc the
# build is already done, so it emits no JavaScript and the image ends up with
# a dist folder full of .d.ts files and nothing to run. The **/ form matters
# because the file lives at apps/api/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo, and Docker matches
# patterns against the whole path rather than the basename.
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo
